sources_check.out — every quote and figure from outside this reel's own runs, re-read at
the source by Claude, 2026-10-09. PDF text extracted with PyMuPDF.

THE ESSAY (Sai's reference)
- Alexandru Gherghe, "A Lot Of Unit Tests Are Worse Than Useless", 2026-07-13, "8 min read".
  https://alexgherghe.com/articles/some-unit-tests-are-useless.html — saved as
  research/gherghe-2026-07-13.html (26,722 B, fetched 2026-10-09).
  Quotes used:
  · "All this test does is to mirror the implementation of the method, not the behavior of
    the method."                                                               → B01 (paraphrased)
  · "mocking that in tests leaves you wide open to bugs in the queries or broken
    assumptions about what would be returned."                                → B02 ("broken assumptions about what would be returned")
  · "since refactoring means that we need to rewrite the tests anyways, what assurance do
    they give anymore?"                                                       → B03 (paraphrased, "He asks")
  · "Where unit tests genuinely shine is in testing pure logic" … "a pricing calculator
    that applies discounts based on a set of rules"                           → B06
  · "test behavior, not implementation."                                       → DESCRIPTION
  · "who is testing who?"                                                      → title, B09
  The BookService and BookServiceTest code: the Java listing in the essay, ported to
  Python in evidence/bookservice/ (same five collaborators, same calls, same asserts).

SPOTIFY (cited by the essay)
- André Schaffer, "Testing of Microservices", Spotify Engineering, 2018-01-11.
  https://engineering.atspotify.com/2018/01/testing-of-microservices
  "we should focus on Integration Tests, have a few Implementation Detail Tests and even
  fewer Integrated Tests."                                                     → B06

RESEARCH
- Inozemtseva & Holmes, "Coverage Is Not Strongly Correlated with Test Suite
  Effectiveness", ICSE 2014, DOI 10.1145/2568225.2568271 (Crossref); PDF
  https://cs.ubc.ca/~rtholmes/papers/icse_2014_inozemtseva.pdf, abstract:
  "we generated 31,000 test suites for five systems consisting of up to 724,000 lines of
  source code" … "We found that there is a low to moderate correlation between coverage
  and effectiveness when the number of test cases in the suite is controlled for" …
  "coverage, while useful for identifying under-tested parts of a program, should not be
  used as a quality target".                                                   → B04, B07
- Just, Jalali, Inozemtseva, Ernst, Holmes & Fraser, "Are Mutants a Valid Substitute for
  Real Faults in Software Testing?", FSE 2014, DOI 10.1145/2635868.2635929 (Crossref);
  PDF https://homes.cs.washington.edu/~mernst/pubs/mutation-effectiveness-fse2014.pdf,
  abstract: "Our experiments used 357 real faults in 5 open-source applications that
  comprise a total of 321,000 lines of code" … "The results show a statistically
  significant correlation between mutant detection and real fault detection,
  independently of code coverage."                                            → B04
